It's fixed. Nginx was still sending traffic to the old PHP container's IP.

**Cause:** Every deploy recreates the `php` container, and Docker gives it a new IP. Nginx had looked up `php` once at startup and kept using that address. After a deploy it was still sending requests to the old one: `docker inspect` showed php had moved from `.5` to `.7`, but nginx was still hitting `.5`. That's where the 502s came from.

I also checked whether php-fpm was crashing, but its logs were clean.

**Fix:** I replaced the `set $php_fpm php:9000` / `fastcgi_pass $php_fpm` setup with an upstream block that keeps re-resolving the name:

```nginx
resolver 127.0.0.11 valid=1s;

upstream php_fpm {
    zone php_fpm 64k;
    server php:9000 resolve;
}
```

`127.0.0.11` is Docker's built-in DNS server. With `resolve` on the server line, nginx looks up `php` again as records expire and picks up the new IP on its own.

**Verified:** I recreated the php container twice. There were no 502s either time, and nginx didn't need a reload.

The `resolve` option on upstream servers needs nginx 1.27.3 or newer (or nginx Plus). It works on the version you're running now, but don't pin an older nginx image or this will break. Also, `valid=1s` makes nginx re-check DNS every second, which is cheap against Docker's local DNS. I can raise it to 5–10s if you want a bit less churn.

